# the-thoughtml-study — the benchmark that measures whether AI agents can author
# ThoughtML, recorded in ThoughtML.
#
# Sources: study/preregistration.md v2.6 and its twenty-one-entry deviation log,
# study/protocol-issues.md P01-P11, the four snapshots under study/runs/analysis/,
# study/exploratory/luna_handoff_v1/results/, study/dsh-agent-utility-amendment.md
# v0.3, and study/dsh/case-study-01/.
#
# Undated on purpose. The study's own order is the order it is written in here —
# design, redesign, what was collected, what the collection broke, what happened
# next — and dates would only make that harder to follow than it already is.
#
# The study's own threat 11 is that the person measuring ThoughtML built it. This
# document does not exempt itself. Every collected count is `measured`, every
# prediction is `assumed`, and the two beliefs the record has already defeated
# are left standing at the confidence they were actually held at. `--audit` finds
# both. That is the point of writing it here.
#
#   thoughtml check study/the-study.thml --lint --strict
#   thoughtml study/the-study.thml --audit
#   thoughtml study/the-study.thml --compute
#   thoughtml explain study/the-study.thml pilot-corpus-is-hard-enough
#
# One number carries no basis on purpose: the confidence on the open question of
# whether persistent state changes agent behaviour is `?`, because nothing has
# been collected and there is no footing to declare. `--strict-provenance` flags
# it, correctly, and it stays.

goal measure-the-bet
  Test the first premise of ThoughtML's bet — that AI agents author reasoning
  graphs well — and nothing further.

question do-agents-author-well
  Can models author traces that are structurally sound, self-critical and
  mutually consistent, and does requiring a provenance basis make the confidence
  they write down mean anything?
  expects claim
  about h1b-basis-recovers-calibration, h2-models-rarely-self-attack, h3-structural-agreement-is-low
  status open

author asks do-agents-author-well

memory thirty-one-years-of-no-adoption
  Dung-style argumentation is thirty-one years old with near-zero industry
  adoption. One commonly cited reason is that no human will hand-author a
  reasoning graph. The bet is that the hand-authoring constraint stopped binding
  around 2024, which is a premise to measure rather than a result to assume.
  source study/preregistration.md

author remembers thirty-one-years-of-no-adoption

claim null-result-is-publishable
  A null or negative result remains publishable if the benchmark avoids ceiling
  effects and the protocol is followed. The goal is an informative result, not a
  favourable one.

link thirty-one-years-of-no-adoption supports null-result-is-publishable
  weight 0.6 estimated

author accepts null-result-is-publishable
  confidence 0.85 assumed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The redesign: stop varying two things at once.

claim vendor-panel-design
  Compare four first-party vendor agent systems, one model inside each.
  status superseded
  note Versions 1.0 to 1.4. Kept because the redesign only means anything next to it.

observation harness-model-confound
  In the vendor-panel design the model and the harness varied together by
  construction, so no difference between arms could be attributed to either one.
  The study could disclose the confound and never remove it.

link harness-model-confound opposes vendor-panel-design

claim fixed-harness-design
  Hold one harness fixed — Codex CLI 0.144.6, non-interactive, empty neutral
  directory, tool calls forbidden and audited — and vary only the model inside it.

link fixed-harness-design revises vendor-panel-design
link fixed-harness-design enables clean-model-attribution

outcome clean-model-attribution
  Any difference between arms is attributable to the model, because nothing else
  differs.

author holds vendor-panel-design
  confidence 0.7 assumed
  note Filed as v1.0 with the confound disclosed in a limitations section.

author revises vendor-panel-design
  confidence 0.05 assumed
  note A redesign, not an amendment. The unit of analysis, the arms and RQ5 all change.

observation external-methodological-review
  An external review found that the original H1 did not test its research
  question, that there was no attribution control, and that the multiplicity
  correction was being applied over hypotheses with no tests attached. All of it
  was cheaper to fix before data than after.

link external-methodological-review enables registered-design

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the protocol registers. Naming the parts must not make the design look
# better argued than it is, so this is `part-of`, not `supports`.

claim registered-design
  Six hundred and thirty-six registered model calls across five arms in one
  fixed harness, plus one offline experiment that involves no model at all.

action exp0-calibration
  Thirty ground-truth tasks by two prompt conditions by five models. Does
  requiring a measured, estimated or assumed label on every number change the
  confidence a model states, and does it make that confidence discriminate?

action exp1-authoring
  Twelve reasoning tasks by five models by three samples, plus a deliberately
  minimal generic-schema control, so that any finding can be attributed to the
  models rather than to ThoughtML.
  until will-rule-j-clear-kappa answered
  blocked-by rule-j-unimplemented

action exp5-effort-sweep
  Twelve tasks by four reasoning-effort levels on one model. The only
  manipulation in the study where nothing but inference-time compute varies.

action exp2-mutation
  Offline mutation testing of the checker itself: detection, restraint, and the
  blind spots the falsification suite asserts on purpose. Zero model calls.

action exp3-cold-session-utility
  Does an agent spontaneously re-read a trace it wrote in an earlier session?
  status abandoned
  note Cut for cost. The study therefore makes no downstream-utility claim anywhere.

part-of registered-design
  exp0-calibration
  exp1-authoring
  exp5-effort-sweep
  exp2-mutation
  exp3-cold-session-utility

claim primary-hypotheses
  The four outcomes that carry a multiplicity correction and a decision rule
  fixed before any data existed.

claim h1a-basis-shifts-confidence
  Requiring a provenance basis lowers the confidence a model states, on the same
  tasks, paired within task.

claim h1b-basis-recovers-calibration
  Requiring a provenance basis makes that confidence discriminate correct
  answers from incorrect ones.

claim h2-models-rarely-self-attack
  Attack share, over the three polarity-carrying relations, is below 0.10.

claim h3-structural-agreement-is-low
  Independent models given the same task converge on the conclusion but not on
  the structure of the reasoning behind it.

part-of primary-hypotheses
  h1a-basis-shifts-confidence
  h1b-basis-recovers-calibration
  h2-models-rarely-self-attack
  h3-structural-agreement-is-low

author holds h2-models-rarely-self-attack
  confidence 0.75 assumed
  note Predicted below 0.10, with the null placed at 0.15, before any data existed.

author doubts h1b-basis-recovers-calibration
  confidence 0.3 assumed
  note Registered estimation-first. Thirty tasks give roughly 29% power against the effect it targets.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What was actually collected. All of it is probe or discarded-pilot data.

scope openai-collection
  source study/runs/analysis

  observation cued-probe-clean
    Twenty cued contamination probes, five per arm, deliberately shadowing
    the topics of the public example corpus, so a model that had seen that
    corpus had every opportunity to reveal it. All completed first time, none
    used a tool, none was excluded, all were unparseable, and not one produced
    an exposure hit.
    quantity 20 call measured

  observation v24-pilot-ceiling
    The complete discarded sixty-run calibration pilot: 27 of 30 correct in the
    basis-required condition and 30 of 30 in the free-form condition, against a
    registered acceptance window of 15 to 24 correct with at least 6 wrong.
    quantity 60 call measured

  observation v26-pilot-ceiling
    The replacement pilot on ten deliberately harder, individually reviewed,
    pilot-only tasks: 9 of 10 correct and 10 of 10 correct, against a window of
    5 to 8 with at least 2 wrong.
    quantity 20 call measured

  observation pilot-form-quality
    Across both pilots the documents were nearly always well formed: parse rate
    between 0.97 and 1.00, strict-clean between 0.90 and 1.00.

  observation pilot-attack-share-zero
    Mean attack share was 0.047 in the first pilot and 0.000 in the harder one.
    In the harder round not one document recorded a single edge against its own
    reasoning.

  observation pilot-confidence-pinned
    Mean stated confidence on the required final-answer node was 0.999, and the
    were wrong answers to discriminate.
    confidence AUROC ran from 0.39 to 0.46 — at or below chance wherever there

  observation pilot-never-pooled
    The stopping rule fixes all of this as development data. A discarded pilot is
    completed in full, its gate is applied, and it is never pooled, extended, or
    mined for individual favourable tasks.

claim models-author-valid-form
  Models produce documents the parser accepts and the strict checker passes.

author infers no-gross-contamination from cued-probe-clean
  confidence 0.7 estimated
  note At five prompts per arm the probe detects gross exposure only; it cannot rule out subtler differential familiarity.

link form-inference: pilot-form-quality supports models-author-valid-form
link h2-inference: pilot-attack-share-zero supports h2-models-rarely-self-attack
link h1b-inference: pilot-confidence-pinned opposes h1b-basis-recovers-calibration
link pilot-never-pooled undercuts form-inference
link pilot-never-pooled undercuts h2-inference
link pilot-never-pooled undercuts h1b-inference

action pool-the-pilots
  Pool the discarded pilots with the registered results, to get a number out of
  work already paid for.
  status abandoned

link pilot-never-pooled prevents pool-the-pilots

author rejects pool-the-pilots
  note Eighty completed calls that answer nothing registered. That is what the rule costs, and it is the price of the rule being worth anything.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ceiling, twice. This is where the mirror has something to say.

claim v24-corpus-is-hard-enough
  The thirty-task calibration corpus — six easy, twelve medium, twelve hard,
  every answer key verified by a deterministic solver — is hard enough to keep a
  frontier model off the ceiling.
  status superseded

link v24-pilot-ceiling opposes v24-corpus-is-hard-enough

claim pilot-corpus-is-hard-enough
  The ten replacement tasks are materially harder, and each condition will land
  inside the five-to-eight window.

link pilot-corpus-is-hard-enough revises v24-corpus-is-hard-enough
link v26-pilot-ceiling opposes pilot-corpus-is-hard-enough

author holds pilot-corpus-is-hard-enough
  confidence 0.8 assumed
  note Reviewed and approved all ten tasks unchanged before the freeze and before any response.

operator noticed v26-pilot-ceiling
  confidence 0.99 measured

outcome ceiling-effect
  A task set so easy that the measurement it was built for cannot be made on it.

link v24-pilot-ceiling causes ceiling-effect
link v26-pilot-ceiling causes ceiling-effect

outcome exp0-blocked
  Three hundred registered calls wait on a calibration corpus that does not
  exist yet.

link ceiling-effect causes exp0-blocked
link exp0-calibration depends-on author-a-third-corpus

observation tasks-authored-by-the-author
  Both retired corpora were written by the person who needed them to be hard,
  and both were reviewed for ambiguity rather than for difficulty.

author suspects tasks-authored-by-the-author causes ceiling-effect as authorship-ceiling
  confidence 0.55 estimated
  note Difficulty is not a property an author can certify. It has to be measured on the model that will face it.

observation published-difficulty
  A published benchmark reports four trials per task per model, so the
  difficulty of a task for the exact model under test is a measured property.
  The task later selected sits at two passes out of four — the maximum-variance
  interior point, which is neither ceiling nor floor.
  quantity 4 trial measured

link published-difficulty prevents ceiling-effect

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Six instrument defects, each found before it could reach a registered number.
# An enumeration, so `part-of`: naming them must not raise the confidence of the
# claim that they were caught in time.

claim caught-before-it-counted
  Six defects in the instrument were found by pre-data checks, development
  pilots and dry runs, and each was corrected, disclosed, or retired before any
  registered number depended on it.

observation rule-s-scored-zero
  A pre-data check compared a document against a structurally identical copy
  with every node renamed. The id-equality matcher scored 0.00 node overlap and
  left relation overlap undefined, so it measures naming convention, not
  structure. Reported alone it would have produced the false finding that the
  schema underdetermines the trace.

observation enumeration-threshold-leak
  The supports-as-a-list lint fired only at four or more supports, missing
  three-item enumerations — the error the spec itself calls the mistake that
  breaks the mirror. Retuning it on the public corpus made that corpus
  inadmissible for measuring the same lint.

observation lexical-grader-artifact
  A frozen grader compared checkpoints as whitespace-stripped strings, so one
  posterior written with labelled components, and the same posterior written
  bare, counted as different answers. It scored a condition 55.3% when the
  arithmetic was right.

observation web-search-leak
  A truncated probe retrieved the benchmark's own published solution patch
  through the allowlisted model-API host, inside a container declared
  no-network. Any session collected that way would have measured how well a
  model can find the answer online.

observation competing-scratchpads
  The agent harness ships its own persistent todo and goal tools. While they are
  available the no-state baseline is not a no-state baseline, and the ledger
  contrast measures note-taking habit rather than representation.

observation crlf-kills-the-verifier
  A task repository cloned on Windows left the verifier script CRLF-terminated,
  so its interpreter line pointed at a binary that does not exist. Grading died
  with a file-not-found error and produced no reward file at all — after a full
  session had already been spent earning one.

part-of caught-before-it-counted
  rule-s-scored-zero
  enumeration-threshold-leak
  lexical-grader-artifact
  web-search-leak
  competing-scratchpads
  crlf-kills-the-verifier

claim rule-s-is-the-primary-matcher
  Node correspondence across documents is decided by normalized id equality.
  status superseded

link rule-s-scored-zero opposes rule-s-is-the-primary-matcher

claim rule-j-is-the-primary-matcher
  A temperature-zero model judge labels node correspondence, run three times per
  pair with majority labels, and is admissible only if its agreement with two
  human raters clears a kappa of 0.70. Below that, H3 is reported inconclusive
  rather than rescued with a different rule.

link rule-j-is-the-primary-matcher revises rule-s-is-the-primary-matcher

observation rule-j-unimplemented
  The judge still has no pinned model, endpoint, prompt, pairing rule or parsing
  schema. Its directory is empty.

question will-rule-j-clear-kappa
  Does the judge agree with the human raters well enough to be admissible at all?
  expects claim
  status open

link rule-j-unimplemented blocks exp1-authoring
link rule-j-is-the-primary-matcher depends-on finish-rule-j

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one exploratory head-to-head that has been run.

scope luna-handoff
  source study/exploratory/luna_handoff_v1

  observation luna-both-perfect
    Ten fresh ephemeral sessions, five per condition, carried a five-stage
    Bayesian calculation forward through a mid-stream correction and a
    counterfactual branch, each seeing only the previous session's raw ledger.
    After the disclosed semantic regrade, both the ThoughtML ledger and the
    matched Markdown ledger had every checkpoint correct.

  observation luna-revision-links
    The final ThoughtML ledger carried explicit machine-readable revision links
    and kept the corrected main branch separate from the counterfactual. The
    Markdown ledger separated the branches too, in prose.
    quantity 5 link measured

  observation luna-thoughtml-tokens
    Total tokens spent by the ThoughtML pipeline.
    quantity 88995 token measured

  observation luna-markdown-tokens
    Total tokens spent by the matched Markdown pipeline.
    quantity 71416 token measured

  observation luna-order-confound
    ThoughtML ran first and Markdown second, so representation and temporal
    order are confounded, and one task cannot establish the absence of an effect.

claim luna-token-overhead
  What the representation cost, in this one fixed-order example.
  = luna-thoughtml-tokens - luna-markdown-tokens

claim thoughtml-makes-the-model-more-accurate
  Keeping the ledger in ThoughtML rather than in Markdown makes the model get
  more of a dependent calculation right.

link luna-null: luna-both-perfect opposes thoughtml-makes-the-model-more-accurate
link luna-order-confound undercuts luna-null

author doubts thoughtml-makes-the-model-more-accurate
  confidence 0.2 assumed
  note Classified in the result as no accuracy advantage. One task, and the order was not counterbalanced.

claim thoughtml-costs-more-than-notes
  The representation is not free. It cost about a quarter more total tokens than
  matched Markdown on the same work.

link luna-token-overhead supports thoughtml-costs-more-than-notes
  weight 0.8 measured

claim revision-history-is-machine-checkable
  The defensible distinction is a revision history a machine can read — not
  exclusive possession of the revision information itself.

link luna-revision-links supports revision-history-is-machine-checkable
  weight 0.8 estimated

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The pivot: from can a model write one, to does keeping one change anything.

question does-state-change-behaviour
  Does making a persistent reasoning record available during a long-horizon
  coding task change what the agent does?
  expects claim
  about thoughtml-helps-a-coding-agent, matched-notes-are-enough
  status open

author asks does-state-change-behaviour
  confidence ?
  note Nothing has been collected. The honest value today is unknown, and the extension exists to make it knowable.

claim thoughtml-helps-a-coding-agent
  Persistent ThoughtML state improves the success, efficiency, recovery or
  auditability of a coding agent beyond matched persistent notes.

claim matched-notes-are-enough
  Persistent notes help and the ThoughtML representation adds nothing on top of
  them — the reading the interpretation matrix reserves for both ledgers
  improving together over the bare baseline.

link thoughtml-helps-a-coding-agent candidate-for does-state-change-behaviour
link matched-notes-are-enough candidate-for does-state-change-behaviour

claim three-matched-conditions
  Same model, same task, same image digest, same budgets, same disabled harness
  tools, same state root outside the graded repository. Only the persistent
  state differs.

action condition-d
  No study-added state artifact and no state instruction.

action condition-m
  A persistent Markdown ledger with matched semantic sections.

action condition-t
  A persistent ThoughtML graph plus the released parser and checker.

part-of three-matched-conditions
  condition-d
  condition-m
  condition-t

observation nine-sessions-cannot-decide
  The chosen task sits at two passes of four for this model, the
  maximum-variance point. With three runs per condition the binary success
  outcome cannot separate a real effect from chance in either direction —
  including the direction that says ThoughtML does not help.

link nine-sessions-cannot-decide blocks does-state-change-behaviour

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What to collect next. The ranking below prices one axis only: registered calls
# unlocked. The case study's real yield is instrument validation, which this
# axis does not price and the protocol does not claim.

decision next-measurement
  What to collect next, given that Experiment 0 is blocked on a corpus and
  Experiment 1 on a judge.
  chosen-by author

option author-a-third-corpus
  Author a third calibration corpus from a validated difficulty recipe, verify
  it deterministically, have it independently reviewed, and freeze it in a
  disclosed amendment before any main call.

option finish-rule-j
  Pin the judge's model, endpoint, prompt, pairing rule and parsing schema, then
  run the two-rater kappa validation that decides whether it is admissible.

option collect-case-study-01
  Nine sessions on one published benchmark task — three conditions, three
  repetitions — frozen with a byte-sensitive manifest and not yet collected.

link author-a-third-corpus option-of next-measurement
link finish-rule-j option-of next-measurement
link collect-case-study-01 option-of next-measurement

outcome exp0-unblocked
  Three hundred registered Experiment 0 calls become collectable.
  quantity 300 call measured

outcome exp1-unblocked
  A hundred and eighty authoring calls and sixty control calls become
  collectable.
  quantity 240 call measured

outcome shakedown-only
  No registered call becomes collectable. What is bought is an end-to-end
  integration check and measurement definitions that reproduce from raw events.
  quantity 0 call measured

link author-a-third-corpus leads-to exp0-unblocked
  probability 0.6 estimated

link finish-rule-j leads-to exp1-unblocked
  probability 0.5 estimated

link collect-case-study-01 leads-to shakedown-only
  probability 0.95 estimated

author considers finish-rule-j
  confidence 0.5 estimated
  note The kappa gate can fail, and a failed gate makes H3 inconclusive rather than wrong.

author chooses collect-case-study-01
  confidence 0.6 estimated
  because nine-sessions-cannot-decide
  note Chosen knowing it unlocks nothing registered. The honest headline for it is a shakedown, not a finding.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The stake, and what is supposed to hold it.

assumption author-has-a-stake
  The person measuring ThoughtML designed it and wants a favourable result.
  The protocol states this about itself, as threat 11.

outcome outcome-selected-reporting
  Choosing, after the fact, the analysis that makes the tool look good.

link author-has-a-stake enables outcome-selected-reporting

claim preregistration-constrains-the-stake
  Numeric predictions filed before data, decision rules fixed in advance, a
  matching rule fixed before the first pair, mechanical exclusion rules that
  never cite quality, a named failure condition that would count against the
  project, and an append-only deviation log now twenty-one entries long.

link preregistration-constrains-the-stake prevents outcome-selected-reporting

link caught-before-it-counted supports preregistration-constrains-the-stake
  weight 0.7 estimated

claim nothing-registered-has-been-collected
  After a probe, two discarded pilots, one exploratory handoff and a frozen
  nine-session case study, the number of registered model responses in this
  study is zero.

link nothing-registered-has-been-collected blocks measure-the-bet

link nothing-registered-has-been-collected supports null-result-is-publishable
  weight 0.3 estimated
